          "G176:p0024:b016",
          "G176:p0024:b017"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Every effort starts with some notion of a problem and an approach to dealing with a problem. For example, an enterprise might have the problem of disparate systems that do not communicate and they may have the notion that to address the problem they may implement an Enterprise Resource Planning (ERP) system. This proposition is an example of a premise. This premise in essence presumes an answer to an unstated question – do ERPs actually reduce my problem of all my systems that do not interoperate? Sometimes this premise and the unstated question do not get examined, which can be a critical mistake. In this phase we attempt to develop an understanding of the premise and unstated question or questions that need to be examined to ensure an architecture project is on point.",
        "source_block_ids": [
          "G176:p0024:b019",
          "G176:p0024:b020",
          "G176:p0024:b021",
          "G176:p0024:b022",
